  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Suppose $a,b,c$ are positive numbers and $a,b,c,bc\\ne1$. Prove that\n$$\\log_{bc}a=\\frac{(\\log_b a)(\\log_c a)}{\\log_b a+\\log_c a}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle\\log_{bc}a=\\frac{(\\log_b a)(\\log_c a)}{\\log_b a+\\log_c a}$.\n\nUse base swapping, the product law and a common denominator.",
      "worked": "Using base swapping and the product law,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{bc}a&=\\frac{1}{\\log_a(bc)}\\\\\\log_{bc}a&=\\frac{1}{\\log_a b+\\log_a c}\\\\\\log_{bc}a&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\log_b a}+\\frac{1}{\\log_c a}}\\\\\\log_{bc}a&=\\frac{(\\log_b a)(\\log_c a)}{\\log_b a+\\log_c a}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
